sit Us\nUndergraduate\nMajors\nSpecial Interdisciplinary Major\nMinors\nMicro-Minors\nCombined Degree Programs\nEngaged Learning Opportunities\nDean's Seminars\nLuther Rice Undergraduate Research Fellowship\nRobert Vincent Fellowship Fund\nGW Early College Program\nCareer Services\nProspective Students\nApply to an Undergraduate Program\nCurrent Students\nStudent Awards\nGrad2Grad\nCCAS Academic Advising\nContact\nGraduate\nMaster's Degrees\nDoctoral Degrees\nGraduate Certificates\nProspective Students\nWhy Choose Columbian College?\nApply\nApplication Requirements\nApplication Process\nInternational Applicants\nGraduate Admissions FAQ\nInformation Sessions & Events\nMeet Our Ambassadors\nRequest Information\nAdmitted Students\nNew Student Orientation\nOfficial Transcript Requirements\nAdmitted International Students\nAdmitted Student Events\nCurrent Students\nDegreeMAP\nStudent Handbook\nAcademic Standing\nArbitrary or Capricious Evaluation\nDual Enrollment/Joint Programs\nEnglish for Academic Purposes (EAP)\nIncomplete Grades \"I\"\nLeave of Absence\nRepeating Courses\nRegistration\nStudent Rights, Responsibilities and Academic Integrity\nTime to Degree\nTransfer Credit\nHuman Research Requirements\nMaster's Degree Special Requirements\nMaster's Thesis ETD Submission\nDoctoral Degree Special Requirements\nPhD Defense Procedures\nStudent Forms\nGraduate Student Form Submission\nGraduation\nAttending Commencement\nMaster\u2019s Students\nMaster's Degree Along the Way Students\nCertificate Students\nDoctoral Students\nPh.D. Clearance Deadlines\nGraduation FAQs\nStudent Newsletter\nThe Bouchet Society\nCurrent Graduate Student Events\nGraduate Student Appreciation Week\nThree Minute Thesis (3MT)\nFunding & Cost\nMaster's and PsyD Funding\nMinority-Serving Institution Award\nDoctoral Funding\nAdvanced PhD Funding\nExternal Funding Opportunities\nTuition and Living Expenses\nTuition & Cost for International Students\nContact\nResearch\nFaculty Research\nFaculty Out Front\nResearch Support\nFaculty Grants and Awards\nFaculty Research Conversations\nStudent Research\nHow to Do Undergraduate Research\nCenters & Institutes\nNews & Events\nFeatured Stories\nPress Announcements\nPublications\nMagazine\nSpotlight E-Magazine\nGetting Press\nJuly 2026\nNoteworthy\nNoteworthy Archive\nFaculty Books\nColumbian College Viewbook\nFaculty & Staff Recognition\nEvents\nDean\u2019s Conversation Series\nCommunications Resources\nAlumni & Giving\nGiving Priorities\nWays to Give\nAlumni & Donor Events\nStories of Impact\nAlumni Books\nMonumental Alumni\nAdvisory Councils\nEmeriti Members\nAlumni & Giving Team\nHome\nAbout\nDepartments & Programs\nLeft navigation\nAbout\nVision & Mission\nAdministration\nDepartments & Programs\nAcademic Chairs & Program Directors\nCareer Services\nFaculty Resources, Policies & Committees\nGlobal Engagement\nDiversity, Equity & Inclusion\nContact Us\nDepartments and Programs\nColumbian College offers students a unique opportunity to explore and pursue a wide range of academic interests across our diverse fields of study.\nWith a faculty comprised of accomplished scholars and facilities that encourage innovation, students in our 42 academic departments and programs make the most of their #OnlyatGW experiences.\nView Academic Chairs & Program Directors\nDepartments and Programs by Alphabet\nAfricana Studies\nAmerican Studies\nAnatomy & Cell Biology\nAnthropology\nArt History (Undergraduate)\nArt History (Graduate)\nArt Therapy\nBiochemistry & Molecular Medicine\nBiological Sciences\nBiomedical Sciences\nChemistry\nClassical Acting\nClassical & Near Eastern Languages & Civilizations\nContemporary Art Practices\nCorcoran School of the Arts & Design\nDance\nData Science\nEast Asian Languages & Literatures\nEconomics\nEnglish\nEnglish for Academic Purposes\nEnvironmental Studies\nFilm Studies\nFine Arts\nForensic Psychology\nForensic Sciences\nGeography & Environment\nGeological Sciences\nGraphic Design\nHistory\nHuman Services & Social Justice\nInteraction Design (Undergraduate)\nInteraction Design (Graduate)\nInterior Architecture (Undergraduate)\nInterior Architecture (Graduate)\nJudaic Studies\nLanguage Center\nLinguistics\nMathematics\nMedia & Public Affairs\nMuseum Studies\nMusic\nNew Media Photojournalism (Graduate)\nOrganizational Sciences & Communication\nPeace Studies\nPhilosophy\nPhotojournalism (Undergraduate)\nPhysics\nPolitical Science\nProfessional Psychology\nPsychological & Brain Sciences\nPublic Policy & Public Administration\nWorld Religions\nRomance, German & Slavic Studies\nSociology\nSpeech, Language & Hearing Sciences\nStatistics\nTheatre\nUniversity Writing Program\nWomen's, Gender, & Sexuality Studies\n\"The advantage of studying in a field like [American Studies] is that you can be creative with your work and move in so many exciting directions.\"\nJacklin Bolduan\nPhD '25, American Studies\nDepartments and Programs by Field of Study\nArts & Design\nFrom its world-class museums to the international diversity of its residents, D.C. is a vibrant city bursting with arts and culture\u2014and GW\u2019s Columbian College is right in the thick of it. The renowned Corcoran School of the Arts and Design is a cornerstone of the D.C. community and, as a school within Columbian College, it continues to nurture students and practitioners in the creative fields while also enriching those pursuing other areas of study.\nArts & Design Programs\nArt History (Undergraduate)\nArt History (Graduate)\nClassical Acting\nContemporary Art Practices\nDance\nFilm Studies\nFine Arts\nGraphic Design\nInteraction Design (Undergraduate)\nInteraction Design (Graduate)\nInterior Architecture (Undergraduate)\nInterior Architecture (Graduate)\nMuseum Studies\nMusic\nNew Media Photojournalism (Graduate)\nPhotojournalism (Undergraduate)\nTheatre\nClinical Centers and Programs\nAt Columbian College, expert educators and top practitioners are working to advance the science and practice of a wide variety of therapeutic specialties. Our graduate-level programs provide hands-on experience and emphasize clinical theories and methods that prepare students to tackle the underlying challenges facing individuals, communities and society as a whole.\nClinical Centers and Programs\nArt Therapy Clinic\nCenter Clinic\nMeltzer Center\nSpeech and Hearing Center\nHumanities\nPolicy and the sciences determine what we do; the humanities determine who we are. Our faculty and students are engaged in research and learning that reaches around the world in areas such as languages, religious understanding and philosophical exploration. In today\u2019s 21st-century global society, there is a growing need for individuals steeped in these fields of study.\nHumanities\nAfricana Studies\nAmerican Studies\nClassical & Near Eastern Languages & Civilizations\nEast Asian Languages & Literatures\nEnglish\nJudaic Studies\nLanguage Center\nPeace Studies\nPhilosophy\nWorld Religions\nRomance, German & Slavic Studies\nUniversity Writing Program\nWomen's, Gender, & Sexuality Studies\nMathematics & Statistical Analysis\nOur quantitative programs are preparing the next generation of analytical experts with both the practical and theoretical knowledge needed to extract actionable solutions through sophisticated processes and techniques. From positions in the Department of Homeland Security to the nation\u2019s high-tech corridors, these skills translate into a variety of high-demand career paths.\nMathematics & Statistical Analysis\nBiostatistics\nData Science\nEconomics\nMathematics\nStatistics\nNatural & Forensic Sciences\nLook inside our labs and you\u2019ll find students working with faculty to develop revolutionary carbon dioxide-free methods to reduce global warming, searching for ways to mitigate the diminishing honey bee population or exploring supermassive black holes to gain a better understanding of the origins of our universe. Our innovative Science and Engineering Hall is further propelling learning and discovery through interdisciplinary collaboration and the use of cutting-edge technology.\nNatural & Forensic Sciences\nAnatomy & Cell Biology\nBiochemistry & Molecular Medicine\nBiological Sciences\nBiomedical Sciences\nChemistry\nEnvironmental Studies\nForensic Sciences\nGeological Sciences\nPhysics\nPolicy & Public Affairs\nOur location in the heart of the nation\u2019s capital\u2014at the very intersection of diplomacy, legislative affairs and executive action\u2014makes Columbian College the place for immersion in the study of policy, journalism and public administration. Our access, engagement, and proximity are unparalleled and reflective of our programs, which are ranked among the best in the country.\nPolicy & Public Affairs\nMedia & Public Affairs\nPublic Policy & Public Administration\nSocial & Behavioral Sciences\nThe social and behavioral sciences touch a broad swath of our daily lives. For example, geography students are mapping immigration trends in urban areas, students of anthropology are unlocking the patterns of human aging and psychology students are exploring the \u201cnature vs. nurture\u201d theories behind childhood obesity. Their studies are helping them gain a clearer understanding of our past in order to provide better insight into our future.\nSocial & Behavioral Sciences\nAnthropology\nEconomics\nForensic Psychology\nGeography & Environment\nHistory\nHuman Services & Social Justice\nLinguistics\nOrganizational Sciences & Communication\nPolitical Science\nProfessional Psychology\nPsychological & Brain Sciences\nSociology\nColumbian College of Arts & Sciences\nPhillips Hall\n801 22nd St. NW\nWashington, DC 20052\n202-994-6210\nContact Us\nUndergraduate Admissions\nGraduate Admissions\nGiving Priorities\nCampus Advisories\nEO/Nondiscrimination Policy\nWebsite Privacy Notice\nContact GW\nAccessibility\nTerms of Use\nCopyright\nReport a Barrier to Accessibility", "links": [["Skip to main content", "https://columbian.gwu.edu